.TH CSTR_SUBSTRING 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cstr_substring \- create a new string from a sub-range, and call _ccol_fatal_err() on failure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

cstr result = cstr_substring(s, start, len);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a GNU C statement expression that calls cstring_substring()
and calls _ccol_fatal_err() when the result is NULL, which happens only
when an allocation failed. The new cstring is the value of the macro, so
you can use it directly inside an expression.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s      Source cstr
start  Zero-based start index
len    Number of characters to include
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new cstr that holds the sub-range that you asked for
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}), so it needs GCC or
Clang.
A range that runs past the end of the string is clamped at the end
instead of being rejected.
You must destroy the cstring that this macro returns.
The macro evaluates each of s, start and len exactly once, so the failure
message reports the start and the length that the call really used.
If you want to handle an error yourself, call cstring_substring()
directly.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cstr_construct(src, "Hello, world!");
cstr sub = cstr_substring(src, 7, 5);   /* "world" */
printf("%s\en", cstr_c_str(sub));
cstr_destroy(sub);
cstr_destroy(src);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstring_substring (3),
.BR cstr_copy (3),
.BR cstr_split (3)
